INT. FUNICULAR STATION - OPERATOR'S BOOTH - NIGHT

Sodium light. Rain on tin. A brass lever the size of a forearm.

ODALYS VANTERPOOL, 40s, wet wool coat, hair pinned too tight. She has a cassette recorder. She sets it on the control desk.

Presses PLAY.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape, hiss)
If you're hearing this, I'm already on the car.

ODALYS
I know.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
Don't talk to the tape, Odalys. You hate it when people talk to the tape.

ODALYS
Then stop being so sure of me.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
Three days. That's how long I watched the ledger. Three days of numbers that didn't breathe right.

ODALYS
You said nobody would check the cable inspections.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
Nobody did. That was the point. Brakes on the upper car were signed off in March. By a man who died in January.

ODALYS
Say it plainer.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
The upper car won't hold on the grade. Not with a full load. Not tonight.

ODALYS
There's no load. It's midnight.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
There's a load. Look at the window.

She doesn't look.

ODALYS
I'm not looking.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
Look.

Lights. Halfway up the mountain. A car, lit yellow, climbing.

ODALYS
Who's on it?

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
The board. All nine. The anniversary ride. You booked it.

ODALYS
I booked it.

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape)
You booked it so I couldn't stop it.

Her hand finds the lever. Cold brass.

ODALYS
Then why leave a tape?

ODALYS (V.O.)
(tape, softer)
Because you'll want to pull it. And you'll want to know why you didn't.

Silence. The tape hisses. Rain.

The car crests the grade.

ODALYS
Forgive me.

She pulls the lever.

A shriek of steel from the dark. Then a flash, white, enormous, the whole mountain jumping.

The window blows in.
